{
  "description": "Segment-level vectors for contracts/types.md section 1 CategoryId and section 13 (contract 1.4.0, ADR-067). Both the TypeScript and the Rust implementation load this one file. Every segment is written with JSON unicode escapes so no editor can turn a decomposed form into a composed one. Each row has applies_to, a list of the checks the row is about: 'segment' means a single path segment checked by section 13 (character set, NFC, leading and trailing dot, reserved device names, non-empty, no slash); 'category' means a CategoryId checked by section 1 (the segment rules plus at most 64 UTF-8 bytes). expect is the answer for EACH check listed in applies_to: accept means the segment passes every listed check, refuse means it fails every listed check. A check not listed in applies_to must ignore the row (for example the 65-byte rows are refused as a CategoryId but are legal path segments, because section 13 limits only the whole relPath to 240 bytes). Implementations must select rows by applies_to, never by the rule field; rule and why are explanations for people.",
  "unicode_version_used_to_verify": "15.0.0",
  "vectors": [
    {
      "segment": "security",
      "expect": "accept",
      "applies_to": [
        "segment",
        "category"
      ],
      "rule": "charset",
      "why": "ordinary ASCII lower case"
    },
    {
      "segment": "Security",
      "expect": "accept",
      "applies_to": [
        "segment",
        "category"
      ],
      "rule": "charset",
      "why": "upper case is allowed (ADR-063)"
    },
    {
      "segment": "a.b-c_d9",
      "expect": "accept",
      "applies_to": [
        "segment",
        "category"
      ],
      "rule": "charset",
      "why": "dot inside, hyphen, underscore, digits"
    },
    {
